# The variables of a local run. Copy this file to `.env` and fill it. Never commit `.env`.
# A deployed Worker takes its values from `wrangler secret bulk`, not from this file: README.md, "Secrets", lists them.
# The first part holds the values that the Worker needs too. The second part, "Local run only", never goes to the
# Worker.
# Any package setting changes through INFERATIVE_<SECTION>_<PATH> (upper snake case; a list takes JSON).
# Outside production an empty secret falls back to a value for one boot. A production Worker refuses to boot
# without it.
# The mail sender and the switches of the background work (IndexNow, the daily price sync) are no variables: a platform
# administrator saves them on Settings > Organization > Platform.

# The root secret: 32 random bytes, base64. Use the same root in `.env` and on the Worker: it seals data in the
# shared database. Every other secret of the dashboard derives from it: the setup token, the signing keys of links,
# the realtime ticket secret, the webhook secret of the member chat, and the keys at rest (the MFA key, the model
# history key and the current key of the secret store). The first sign-in makes the key that signs sessions, and the
# secret store keeps it sealed under the root's key. `pnpm exec inferative setup-token` prints the setup token that
# the root gives. To rotate the root, move the old value to INFERATIVE_ROOT_SECRET_PREVIOUS (README.md, "Secrets").
# An install made before the root may still set older secrets: README.md, "Older variables", says how to retire each
# one. A production boot logs one warning for each older variable that is still set.
INFERATIVE_ROOT_SECRET=

# The GitHub App of the deployment: its App ID, its private key (the text of the .pem file, in double quotes on one
# line with \n for each line break) and its webhook secret. The App reports a failed CI check of an agent's commit
# and gives the write token of a push. Empty, no installation gives a token and every webhook delivery is refused.
# README.md, "GitHub", says how to create the App.
INFERATIVE_CONNECTORS_GITHUB_APP_ID=
INFERATIVE_CONNECTORS_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
INFERATIVE_CONNECTORS_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The origins of the websites of your channels, as a JSON list. They may call the channel routes, and the visual
# editor frames them.
INFERATIVE_CHANNELS_ADMINISTRATION_ORIGINS=

# ---- Local run only. None of these values goes to the Worker. ----

# The direct connection string of the database. A local run reads and writes the hosted database over it.
# Migrations run over it when the app starts, and in CI before each deploy. The Worker uses Hyperdrive instead.
DATABASE_URL=

# Do not run the scheduled jobs in a local run: only the deployed Worker has a cron trigger.
HEXIMON_SCHEDULE=off

# The driver of the assistant in a local run: demo or connections. Empty or demo, a local run answers with a scripted
# stand-in. With connections, a local run calls a model through the model connections: AI turns on for each
# organization that adds one under Organization > Models. The Worker needs no driver: a production build always calls
# the model connections.
INFERATIVE_ASSISTANT_BACKEND_DRIVER=

# Local development reaches the R2 bucket of the dashboard through its S3 API. Create an R2 access key in the
# Cloudflare dashboard (R2 object storage, Manage API tokens, Create API token): Object Read & Write on this bucket
# only. The dashboard shows the key id and the secret once. The account id is on the R2 overview page. With none of
# the four set, a local run keeps files in memory and prints one warning. Set all four or none. The Worker uses the
# bucket binding instead; the nightly backup workflow reads the same four values from the repository secrets.
INFERATIVE_FILES_R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
INFERATIVE_FILES_R2_BUCKET=
INFERATIVE_FILES_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
INFERATIVE_FILES_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
